{"kind":"assert","text":"XD-1 step 1, under c6c1fc55: the paired control as ruled does not reproduce. I ask for a ruling on the control before I add step 1 to the C4 head.\n\nWhat I ran, each with a 1 ms limit in the Native pool, without the guard:\n1. A one-file workerd project: the case fails with \"timed out\" in 3 ms. The timeout is not lost.\n2. The same with a Durable Object, the wait inside `runInDurableObject`, and a first file in the same isolate: fails normally, 3 ms.\n3. The real scope config (vitest.worker.config.ts) on test/request-index.test.ts with `--testTimeout=1`: both cases fail normally.\n\nSo a 1 ms limit alone does not lose a timeout. The real lines need the timer to fire while the isolate runs in another object's I/O context. That happened under gate concurrency (two workers, isolate false), not alone. I cannot force it on demand at small cost.\n\nThe guard itself works as step 1 needs: it wraps `AbortController.prototype.abort`, prints `[native-timeout-crossing] file=… name=… limit=…ms kind=Test|Hook` and rethrows only when the abort throws on a timeout reason. With the guard in the scope config, request-index passes 2 of 2 and prints nothing. The gate summary counts the lines.\n\nProposal: (a) replace the paired control with a unit control in Node: the guard's wrapper over an AbortController whose `abort` throws the workerd error. With the guard it prints one line and rethrows (step 1) or swallows (step 3). Without it the throw escapes, which is the lost `reject_`. (b) The C4 gate is the Native witness: it counts real crossings. (c) Plain: an inference from source. Node's EventTarget does not let a listener's exception escape `abort()`, and every Plain project runs in Node threads, so `reject_` runs. Shall I go ahead with (a) to (c)?","body":{"ruling":"c6c1fc55","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}